---
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  timeline
  title="How We Build Our Calculators"
  description={`The methods, tests and sources behind every ${SITE.name} calculator, and how we keep tax figures up to date.`}
>
  <p>
    Every {SITE.name} calculator follows the same process before it is published. This page explains it, so you can
    judge how far to rely on our results.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Standard formulas, shown on the page</h2>
  <p>
    We use the standard method lenders and textbooks use for each calculation, and every calculator has a "How this is
    calculated" box with the exact formula. For example, our loan calculator uses the standard amortisation formula,
    with a monthly rate of APR ÷ 12.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Full precision, rounded only for display</h2>
  <p>
    Calculations run at full precision. We round only when we show a figure, to the nearest cent or penny. On a loan,
    the final payment is adjusted so the balance ends at exactly zero. Lenders who round each payment first may differ
    from us by a few cents or pence in total.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Tested against known answers</h2>
  <p>
    Each calculator's maths is covered by automated tests with known correct answers, including edge cases such as a 0%
    interest rate, a one-month term, extra payments and very large values. We also compare at least three results with
    a trusted independent calculator before publishing, and the tests run again before every update goes live.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Official sources for every tax figure</h2>
  <p>We take tax bands, allowances and legal limits only from official government sources:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>United States: IRS.gov and SSA.gov</li>
    <li>United Kingdom: GOV.UK and HMRC</li>
    <li>Canada: canada.ca and the Canada Revenue Agency (coming soon)</li>
    <li>Australia: the Australian Taxation Office (coming soon)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Every tax calculator shows which tax year it uses.</p>

  <h2>5. Updated when the rules change</h2>
  <p>
    We review each country's figures when its tax year changes: the US and Canada in January, the UK in April and
    Australia in July. Each guide shows the date it was last updated.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Your data stays on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Calculations run in your browser. The numbers you enter are never sent to us. See our
    <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
  <p>
    Please <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>. We check every report and fix confirmed errors as a priority.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
